<Guide lang="en" slug="flooring-options-spain" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="renovatie" ctaTitle="A new floor for your home?" ctaText="Send us photos of the current floor and the number of square metres. You will get advice on the best floor for your home and a price including laying.">
  <h2 id="spanish">Why floors are different in Spain</h2>
  <p>A floor in the Axarquía has to cope with different things than one in the UK or Northern Europe. It has to stay cool in summer, it gets sand and water from the beach or the pool, and in old village houses and fincas the floor often sits directly on the ground with no crawl space. Damp from the ground then rises. That is why almost every Spanish home has a stone or ceramic floor (<em>suelo</em> or <em>solería</em>).</p>
  <p>Still, there is more choice than the standard beige tile. Below we compare all common floors: what they cost, what they are good at and where they do not fit.</p>

  <h2 id="comparison">All floors compared, with prices</h2>
  <p>The price class runs from € (cheap) to €€€€ (expensive), per m² including laying. You get a price for your home in the quote. Removing an old floor and levelling the base come on top (see <a href="#extras">what else to budget for</a>).</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
  <table class="prices">
    <thead><tr><th>Floor</th><th>Price class</th><th>Good at</th><th>Less good at</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Porcelain tile (gres porcelánico)</td><td class="pk">€€</td><td>Strong, waterproof, cool, no maintenance</td><td>Cold in winter, grout lines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Large-format tiles (e.g. 120 × 60 cm or bigger)</td><td class="pk">€€€</td><td>Sleek, few joints, feels spacious</td><td>Needs a very flat base and skilled laying</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Microcement</td><td class="pk">€€€</td><td>Seamless, over old tiles, also on walls</td><td>Scratches, skilled work, resealing every few years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Restoring old terrazzo (grinding and polishing)</td><td class="pk">€</td><td>Cheap, character, lasts decades more</td><td>Broken tiles are hard to match</td></tr>
      <tr><td>New terrazzo</td><td class="pk">€€€€</td><td>Almost indestructible, beautiful</td><td>Price, weight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Barro / terracotta (handmade)</td><td class="pk">€€</td><td>Warm, authentic look</td><td>Porous, stains, needs treating</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Marble and natural stone</td><td class="pk">€€€€</td><td>Luxurious, cool, long-lasting</td><td>Price, sensitive to lemon and vinegar</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Polished or poured concrete</td><td class="pk">€€</td><td>Industrial look, strong</td><td>Hairline cracks, needs a good base</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Click vinyl (SPC/LVT)</td><td class="pk">€</td><td>Quick, waterproof, over tiles, warmer and quieter</td><td>Gets hot in direct sun, less durable than tiles</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Laminate</td><td class="pk">€</td><td>Cheap, wood look</td><td>Swells with damp, not for old houses</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Parquet or solid wood</td><td class="pk">€€€</td><td>Warm and cosy</td><td>Damp, sun and sand, moves with the seasons</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>Small areas, stairs and lots of cutting push the price per m² up. Also allow for a matching skirting (<em>rodapié</em>).</p>

  <h2 id="tiles">Porcelain tiles (gres porcelánico)</h2>
  <p>Spain is one of the world’s biggest tile producers, with factories around Castellón. That makes good tiles affordable here and the choice is huge. Choose <strong>porcelánico</strong> (dense, through-coloured porcelain) rather than ordinary ceramic: it absorbs almost no water, is harder and can also be used outdoors.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Size:</strong> 60 × 60 cm is standard. Large formats (120 × 60, 120 × 120 cm) give fewer joints and a sleek look, but the base must be very flat and laying costs more.</li>
    <li><strong>Wood look:</strong> long tiles with a wood grain give the feel of parquet without the damp problems.</li>
    <li><strong>Colour:</strong> light colours make a room feel bigger and stay cooler. Very dark or very white shows every grain of sand.</li>
    <li><strong>Grout:</strong> choose a narrow joint in a colour close to the tile. Epoxy grout in kitchens and bathrooms stays clean.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="microcement">Microcement</h2>
  <p>Microcement is a thin layer (2 to 3 mm) of cement, resin and pigment, trowelled on by hand in several coats and then sealed. The result is a seamless floor without joints that can continue up walls, stairs and into the shower.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> sleek, modern look; can go over existing tiles, so no demolition and hardly any height difference; no grout to clean; floor and walls in one material.</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> the result depends entirely on the craftsman; the base must not move or hairline cracks appear; more prone to scratches and stains than tiles; the sealant needs refreshing every 2 to 5 years.</li>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> a home you live in yourself, bathrooms and open kitchens. Less so for a busy holiday let with sand and suitcases.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="terrazzo">Terrazzo and old cement tiles</h2>
  <p>Many apartments from the 1960s to 1980s and old village houses have <em>terrazo</em> or <em>granito</em>: cement tiles with marble chips. Or the famous patterned cement tiles (<em>baldosa hidráulica</em>). Many buyers want them out, but that is often a shame.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Restoring:</strong> grinding, filling and polishing makes a dull terrazzo floor look new again (€). Far cheaper than a new floor and no rubble.</li>
    <li><strong>Patterned cement tiles</strong> are sought after today. If you cannot keep them throughout, reuse them in a hallway or as a ‘rug’ in the living room.</li>
    <li><strong>Note:</strong> broken terrazzo tiles are hard to match. Keep a few intact tiles aside during any renovation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="barro">Barro and terracotta</h2>
  <p>Handmade fired-clay tiles, from red to sand-coloured, suit a finca or cortijo. New <em>barro</em> is mid-range (€€). Cleaning and resealing an old barro floor is much cheaper (€).</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> warm, authentic atmosphere; every tile is different; ages beautifully.</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> porous, so oil and wine stains soak in if the floor is not properly treated; needs regular re-treating with oil or wax; less practical in a kitchen.</li>
    <li><strong>Tip:</strong> want the look without the upkeep? There are porcelain tiles with a terracotta finish that are almost impossible to tell from the real thing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="stone">Marble and natural stone</h2>
  <p>Andalusia has its own marble: white marble from Macael (Almería) is found in many villas and on many staircases. Natural stone is luxurious, cool and lasts for generations, but it is expensive (€€€€) and sensitive: acids such as lemon, vinegar and some cleaning products etch polished marble. A honed or brushed finish is less slippery and less vulnerable than high gloss.</p>

  <h2 id="vinyl">Vinyl, laminate and wood</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Click vinyl (SPC or LVT):</strong> the quickest and cheapest way to refresh a dated tiled floor. It goes over the existing tiles, is waterproof, feels warmer and sounds softer. Downsides: in rooms with lots of sun on the floor it can expand and fade, it is less scratch-resistant than tiles and does not last as long. Fine for bedrooms and an apartment you use yourself.</li>
    <li><strong>Laminate</strong> we usually advise against in the Axarquía: damp from the base, a leaking air conditioner or a wet towel makes it swell.</li>
    <li><strong>Parquet or solid wood</strong> can work in a dry, well-insulated house, but has to cope with a damp winter and a dry summer. Choose engineered parquet, never laid straight onto a floor without a damp-proof membrane.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="outdoor">Terrace, roof terrace and pool surround</h2>
  <p>Outdoors, different rules apply. When choosing tiles for a terrace, steps or pool surround, look for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Slip resistance:</strong> choose class C3 (the highest Spanish slip class, <em>clase 3</em>) around the pool and on steps, at least C2 on a terrace.</li>
    <li><strong>Frost resistance</strong> in the mountain villages, where it occasionally freezes in winter.</li>
    <li><strong>Light colour:</strong> dark tiles get so hot in the summer sun that you cannot walk on them barefoot.</li>
    <li><strong>Falls and waterproofing:</strong> on a roof terrace there is a waterproof membrane under the tiles. Laying new tiles without checking it is asking for leaks. See <a href="/en/costs/roof-and-roof-terrace/">roof and roof terrace</a> and <a href="/en/problems/roof-leaks/">roof leaks</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For the pool surround or the whole pool, read <a href="/en/costs/building-a-swimming-pool/">what a swimming pool costs</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="underfloor">Underfloor heating</h2>
  <p>The biggest downside of a stone floor is that it is cold in winter. Underfloor heating solves that, and tiles and microcement conduct heat very well.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Electric heating mat</strong> under the tiles (€€): affordable for a bathroom or one room, but more expensive to run.</li>
    <li><strong>Water-based underfloor heating with a heat pump (aerotermia):</strong> €€€ to €€€€, because a heat pump comes with it. Efficient and comfortable for the whole house, especially when the floor is up anyway during a major renovation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A heat pump heats and cools; see <a href="/en/costs/air-conditioning-and-heat-pumps/">air conditioning and heat pumps</a> for prices.</p>

  <h2 id="choose">Which floor suits your home?</h2>
  <table class="prices">
    <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Our advice</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Holiday let or B&B</td><td>Porcelain in a mid tone with narrow joints: copes with sand, suitcases and cleaning products, and a damaged tile is easy to replace</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1970s apartment with terrazzo</td><td>Have the terrazzo ground and polished, or microcement over it for a modern look</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Old village house with damp floors</td><td>Tackle the damp first, then tiles or barro. No laminate, parquet or vinyl directly on the ground</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Finca or cortijo</td><td>Barro or terracotta-look tiles inside, slip-resistant porcelain outside</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dated floor, small budget</td><td>Click vinyl over the tiles in bedrooms, or tile over tile</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Home with dogs or small children</td><td>Porcelain or terrazzo: scratch-resistant and easy to clean</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lived in all year, major renovation</td><td>Large tiles or microcement with heat-pump underfloor heating</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="extras">What else to budget for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Removing the old floor and rubble:</strong> more expensive in the narrow streets of the white villages, where no skip can be placed and everything goes out by hand.</li>
    <li><strong>Levelling:</strong> needed for large tiles, microcement and vinyl.</li>
    <li><strong>Damp-proofing or drainage</strong> for damp floors: get advice on the cause first, see <a href="/en/problems/damp-and-mould/">damp and mould</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Trimming doors</strong> when the floor rises.</li>
    <li><strong>Moving furniture and protecting the house from dust</strong>, if it is lived in or let furnished.</li>
    <li><strong>VAT:</strong> renovation work on a home can, under conditions, be charged at 10% instead of 21%. See <a href="/en/guides/renovation-costs-spain/">what a renovation costs</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Not living in Spain? A new floor is a job that can easily be done while you are away. Allow 1 to 2 weeks for an average apartment, with photos of every step.</p>
</Guide>
